\section{Retire a Matched Node Only After Its Last Use}

Matching an expression through a producer does not give the replacement
ownership of that producer. Consider a local rewriter for a pure SSA
arithmetic graph. Assume every ordinary instruction is total, roots
represent returned values or other visible uses, and use counts include
each operand occurrence and each root reference. The replacement must
already have passed type, dominance, and semantic checks, and it must not
refer directly or transitively to the result being replaced. Here the
replacement's new nodes are not yet attached to the graph. The rewriter
updates the matched result and removes only now-unreferenced computations.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Old result $o$, validated replacement $r$
\State Attach $r$ and register its operand uses
\To{Count new uses before removing old ones.}
\State Redirect all users and roots from $o$ to $r$
\Let{W}{[o]}
\While{$W$ is not empty}
  \Let{v}{\operatorname{pop}(W)}
  \If{$v$ is removable, attached, and has zero uses}
    \Let{A}{\operatorname{operands}(v)}
    \State Detach $v$ and remove its outgoing operand references
    \To{Only these producers can have lost users.}
    \State Append the producers in $A$ to $W$
  \EndIf
\EndWhile
\end{sudocode}

Use wrapping arithmetic with $\texttt{base}=11$ and $\texttt{index}=5$.
Let $v$ be the shift by two, and let the two returned values be
$\operatorname{add}(\texttt{base},v)$ and $v$ itself. Before rewriting,
$v=20$ has two references and the result pair is $(31,20)$. Replacing
the addition by a scaled-address operation redirects the first root;
removing the original addition takes the use count of $v$ from two to
one. The final pair stays $(31,20)$. If the second root is absent, that
same detachment takes the count to zero and the separate shift can go
away. This is a better selector test than checking just the cost of the
tree rooted at the addition.

At every iteration the counts describe the attached graph, so a node
with zero uses is not needed by any root or attached instruction.
With constant-time use-list operations the cleanup is proportional to
the redirected uses and the operand references of detached instructions,
not the full graph. The restricted procedure will not reclaim a dead
cycle based on counts alone. Nor may it discard an unused operation with
observable effects: such an operation needs an explicit effect root or a
nonremovable designation even if its value has no users.

\section{Retarget Without Leaking Target Choices}

Lewis, Deutsch, and Goldstein's \textit{Clarity MCode: A Retargetable Intermediate Representation for Compilation} illustrates a split between portable type information and target-specific restrictions. Primitive sizes are explicit in MCode, while the backend determines platform alignment and aggregate layout from type information. Its native interface has ABI obligations even though much of the IR is machine independent. Likewise, in a rule system, legality checks must include ABI, register, and addressing constraints whenever generated code exposes them. Changing rule priorities should affect cost, not weaken those checks.

An oriented simplifier needs a terminating strategy or an explicit budget; including both directions of an identity in a greedy rewrite loop can make the compiler cycle. The dossier's Markdown Chapter~10, \textit{Equality Saturation Compiler}, explores keeping multiple equal forms instead of prematurely choosing one, and Chapter~29, \textit{Proof-Carrying IR and Certified Rewrite Contracts}, considers evidence for rule use. Whether alternatives are collected or chosen immediately, check operation schemas, semantic side conditions, and target legality separately. This keeps the addition of a new backend from silently redefining a common operation.
